devfreq: measure load over the sampling window

npu_devfreq_get_dev_status() reported the busy time of the previous whole
second, latched by the 1 Hz load timer, against a constant total. Two reads
inside the same second returned the same sample, so a governor polling
faster than 1 Hz acted repeatedly on stale data, and work starting now was
invisible until the next tick - up to a second later.

Keep a cumulative busy count per core that the timer adds to rather than
resets, and report the difference since the last sample over the interval
that actually elapsed, which is the contract panfrost uses. A job still
running is counted up to the moment of the read, so a core busy throughout
a short window reads as busy rather than idle.

This does not change behaviour on its own: the governor at probe is still
userspace, which does not poll. polling_ms is set so that selecting a
polling governor at runtime is the only write needed.

Signed-off-by: Isaiah DeRose-Wilson <isaiahdw@gmail.com>

--- a/rknpu_devfreq.c
+++ b/rknpu_devfreq.c
@@ -25,6 +25,7 @@
 
